1|0@Libertarian^1440622880#cugppdi:VStarffin$:I don't think the GTFO argument is good either. But you can't claim as part of your argument "they won't let you leave" when we do, in fact, let you leave. Arguments only work if their premises are true. &gt;The NAP doesn't say you get to kill or enslave someone against their will simply for being on your property. I thought the NAP says you can indeed arrest someone for being on your land without your consent. No? Isn't trespassing a crime? And you can quibble whether or not enslavement = imprisonment, but that's not really a persuasive distinction. And if someone is on your property without your consent and won't leave, aren't you allowed to take physical action against them, including deadly force if necessary? I thought this was part of libertarianism.
1|0@Libertarian^1440624244#cugql6h:GravyMcBiscuits$:&gt;But you can't claim as part of your argument "they won't let you leave" when we do I never said anything about them not letting you leave. I said they offer a robust opt-out feature. Allowing one to leave may be a reasonable opt out feature depending on circumstances ... but it isn't a reasonable opt out in most modern societies. &gt;And if someone is on your property without your consent Nope ... the concept of ownership doesn't allow you to just do whatever you want in defense of your property. Whether violence is justified or not is always heavily dependent upon the context. This is not unique to the NAP. There's no "he touched my hair brush therefore I get to shoot him in the face and/or imprison him" rule. &gt;and won't leave Now you have a trickier situation. You've got a good ole fashioned property dispute on your hands and hopefully the actors can come to a peaceful resolution. If they cannot, well hopefully the local community has developed robust methods for settling the issue. If all other avenues for peace are foregone and the 2 actors instead resort to killing each other ... the law of the jungle trumps all in the end. But that has nothing to do with the NAP. None of this is really specific to the NAP in any way though. This is just standard conflict resolution stuff. This is just the nature of human interaction. The NAP is about finding peaceful ways of preventing violence before law-of-the-jungle resolutions prevail.
